<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Governorate of Tucumán</h1><p class="st-meta">1563 CE – 1782 CE · capital Santiago del Estero</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish governorate of northwestern Argentina, a chain of towns linking the River Plate to the silver mines of Upper Peru.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Spanish settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Chile</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-del-estero">Santiago del Estero</a> in 1553, the oldest surviving city in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentina</a>. In 1563 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of Tucumán, Juríes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diaguita">Diaguitas</a>, separating it from Chile and placing it under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Charcas</a>, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-jamaica">Santiago</a> del Estero as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governors founded a series of towns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-de-tucuman">San Miguel de Tucumán</a> in 1565, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1573, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a> in 1582 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador-de-jujuy">San Salvador de Jujuy</a> in 1593. The economy supplied mules, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, textiles and foodstuffs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diaguita">Diaguita</a> and Calchaquí peoples resisted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calchaqui-wars">Calchaquí Wars</a> until the 1660s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-university-of-cordoba">University of Córdoba</a> in 1613, the oldest in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentina</a>, and ran estancias in the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tucumán became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata</a> in 1776. In 1782 the intendancy reform divided it into the intendancies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a> del Tucumán and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> del Tucumán, which later became the provinces of northwestern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentina</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1563 CE – 1782 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:90.81%;width:4.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1563 CE – 1782 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santiago del Estero</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco de Aguirre, Jerónimo Luis de Cabrera, Juan Ramírez de Velasco</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Quechua</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tucumán, Juríes y Diaguitas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1672&amp;focus=governorate-of-tucuman" aria-label="Governorate of Tucumán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2994 3196 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Governorate of Tucumán lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3314 3452L4910 989M3314 3452L2883 2883M3314 3452L3517 3686"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M2883 2883h0M3517 3686h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3314 3452h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3314 3452h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santiago del Estero. <a href="/atlas?year=1672&amp;focus=governorate-of-tucuman">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

